WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.025 --> 00:00:08.591
[SOUND] Thank you very much for taking the
time to come in and speak to us.

00:00:08.591 --> 00:00:13.790
Many of us, are aspiring entrepreneurs, so
we'd really quite like to be like you.

00:00:13.790 --> 00:00:17.168
And, many others, would also like to pitch
to you.

00:00:17.168 --> 00:00:17.840
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:21.687
&gt;&gt; Actually sitting here makes me, gives
me a sense of how intimidating

00:00:21.687 --> 00:00:25.487
that must be, so, I won't, I won't wish it
for much longer.

00:00:25.487 --> 00:00:25.894
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:00:25.894 --> 00:00:29.381
&gt;&gt; And perhaps, perhaps we could, just
start by outlining

00:00:29.381 --> 00:00:32.410
the, the three main topics I'd love to
cover today.

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:37.120
The first is your views on tech and
venture capital trends.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:41.180
The second is, how you assess
entrepreneurial DNA.

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:43.810
And the third is your views on leadership
and your

00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:49.780
leadership experiences, that, that you've
had throughout your, your esteemed career.

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:51.690
And so, if we could perhaps start with
the,

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:53.999
that first, tech trends, and go with
something topical.

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:57.787
You mentioned last month at the
Goldman-Sachs

00:00:57.787 --> 00:01:00.710
conference, that tech was not in a bubble.

00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:03.620
Rather, it was in a mature deployment
phase.

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:05.610
And then the WhatsApp deal happened.

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:08.344
And Mark is on the board of Facebook.

00:01:08.344 --> 00:01:09.894
So I just wanted to ask you, what do you

00:01:09.894 --> 00:01:13.310
think about that deal and how are you
thinking about evaluations?

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:15.689
&gt;&gt; So I, unfortunately, I can't, ten
years, ten years from now

00:01:15.689 --> 00:01:17.739
I can come back and tell you all about the
WhatsApp deal, but

00:01:17.739 --> 00:01:20.035
right now I'm on the, I'm on the, I'm on
the Facebook board

00:01:20.035 --> 00:01:21.900
and I know that you all would not come
visit me in jail.

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:25.942
So I will, I will keep that one to myself.

00:01:25.942 --> 00:01:29.480
so, there's a couple of big things.

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:32.675
So, just in terms of thinking about what
we've been through in the last 20 years in

00:01:32.675 --> 00:01:34.520
Silicon Valley, some people in the room
are

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:36.942
old enough, you may remember there was a
bubble.

00:01:36.942 --> 00:01:41.541
and, it was a fairly big deal, in sort of
1998 to 2000, and there was

00:01:41.541 --> 00:01:45.069
a very profound crash, which was deeply
traumatizing,

00:01:45.069 --> 00:01:47.774
for those of us who went through it.

00:01:47.774 --> 00:01:49.623
And then we went through this extremely

00:01:49.623 --> 00:01:51.920
long period of, basically, you know, years
of

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:56.414
pain followed by then, sort of, what I
think of as, as very slow recovery.

00:01:56.414 --> 00:01:58.074
I think it's actually been an object

00:01:58.074 --> 00:02:00.410
lesson in the psychology of markets and
bubbles.

00:02:00.410 --> 00:02:04.615
I think that, people are much more highly
sensitized to bubbles after a bubble.

00:02:04.615 --> 00:02:09.360
If you could be sensitized to them before
a bubble, you could make a lot more money.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:12.067
But people get highly sensitized and so
there's this phenomena of, of

00:02:12.067 --> 00:02:14.610
trying to close the, the barn door after
the horses have escaped.

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:16.583
And that, that is a lot of what all the

00:02:16.583 --> 00:02:19.840
bubble talk in the last, ten years has
been about.

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:22.370
And so we, we could talk at length about
kinda why I think, in fact,

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:25.932
tech is not now in a bubble and has not
been in a bubble since 2000.

00:02:25.932 --> 00:02:28.973
the, the deeper thing, the more
interesting is this follows a historical

00:02:28.973 --> 00:02:32.107
pattern, which is what I talked about at
the Goldman Conference, which

00:02:32.107 --> 00:02:34.275
is based on the, the best thinker on this
topic is an,

00:02:34.275 --> 00:02:35.866
is an economist named Carlotta Perez,

00:02:35.866 --> 00:02:38.075
who wrote a book called Technological
Revolutions.

00:02:38.075 --> 00:02:39.504
It's probably the single best book.

00:02:39.504 --> 00:02:41.971
Like, that book and The Innovator's
Dilemma are probably the two

00:02:41.971 --> 00:02:44.891
key books that are really critical to
understanding how this industry works.

00:02:45.916 --> 00:02:48.734
And so she describes in her book, she
describes

00:02:48.734 --> 00:02:51.820
a general model for the deployment of new
technologies.

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:54.400
And then how technologies intersect with
financial markets.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:55.290
And so she's got this whole

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:58.052
thing, and it's basically this
multi-generational process.

00:02:58.052 --> 00:03:01.798
And there's what, it's basically these two
big, sort of phases of it.

00:03:01.798 --> 00:03:03.356
There's what's called the installation
phase

00:03:03.356 --> 00:03:05.250
and there's what's called the deployment
phase.

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:07.069
And it turns out in every single case and

00:03:07.069 --> 00:03:10.602
this includes railroads and, like, lots,
electricity and steam engines

00:03:10.602 --> 00:03:13.914
and lots of prior new fundamental
technologies, there's always this

00:03:13.914 --> 00:03:17.760
just gigantic bubble and then crash kind
of halfway through.

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:19.990
And historically that marks the transition
from

00:03:19.990 --> 00:03:21.440
the installation phase to the deployment
state.

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:23.106
The deployment stage, you could argue, is

00:03:23.106 --> 00:03:25.190
where the actual interesting thing, things
happen.

00:03:25.190 --> 00:03:28.538
It's where all the tech-, all the new
technologies actually start to work.

00:03:28.538 --> 00:03:30.280
They actually make it into everybody's
hands.

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:32.260
They actually become cost effective and we
actually

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:34.612
find out how to actually use all these
things.

00:03:34.612 --> 00:03:37.370
And so that's the phase I think we're in,
in now.

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:40.452
You know, without talking about the
Whatsapp deal in particular,

00:03:40.452 --> 00:03:43.262
it is interesting to note that the
companies that people think

00:03:43.262 --> 00:03:47.317
are overvalued today, generally either
have billions of dollars of revenue,

00:03:47.317 --> 00:03:50.368
which was not the case, in, in, in, in the
90s.

00:03:50.368 --> 00:03:52.910
For example, Facebook, people argue
Facebook as an example.

00:03:52.910 --> 00:03:56.750
Facebook went from $0 to $10 billion of
revenue in less than ten years.

00:03:56.750 --> 00:03:59.858
And so that is definitely not what
happened in the 90s.

00:03:59.858 --> 00:04:02.097
The other thing is the companies that
people debate

00:04:02.097 --> 00:04:06.420
today, for the most part, have
extraordinarily high customer, count.

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:08.200
user, user count.

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:10.520
Market sizes have expanded gigantically
and so you've

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:12.346
got these things now that people are
arguing

00:04:12.346 --> 00:04:13.971
about that have, in some cases, a half

00:04:13.971 --> 00:04:16.620
billion users, on their way to a billion
users.

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:18.349
And if people want to take a position that
you

00:04:18.349 --> 00:04:20.501
can have a large scale internet service
that's worth a

00:04:20.501 --> 00:04:22.933
billion users that's not gonna be worth
anything, you, you

00:04:22.933 --> 00:04:25.538
could take that position, I'm not sure you
would recommend it.

00:04:25.538 --> 00:04:27.030
&gt;&gt; Yeah, no, that makes sense.

00:04:27.030 --> 00:04:30.234
When you, as you say, when you look at
the, the cost per user, it's

00:04:30.234 --> 00:04:32.500
actually only $36, which is much, much
less

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:34.630
than in many others for the What'sApp
deal.

00:04:34.630 --> 00:04:38.600
But another thing you, you previously
mentioned was that, MBAs

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:41.730
flocking into the tech sector is a sign of
the bubble.

00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:42.870
So to play devil's advocate.

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:43.620
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:46.080
&gt;&gt; Many of the people here are flocking to
the tech sector.

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:46.210
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:04:46.210 --> 00:04:48.920
&gt;&gt; So, could that, perhaps, be a sign of a
bubble?

00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:50.510
&gt;&gt; So things are heating up.

00:04:50.510 --> 00:04:53.344
And so, [LAUGH] Historically, there's
actually

00:04:53.344 --> 00:04:54.803
been, and I suspect everybody in the

00:04:54.803 --> 00:04:56.119
room knows this, there has been a

00:04:56.119 --> 00:04:59.360
direct correlation between, PE multiples
and, MBAs.

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:01.450
tilting, tilting, tilting into the,
tilting

00:05:01.450 --> 00:05:03.710
into the, the tech industry, for sure.

00:05:03.710 --> 00:05:05.910
So I think something different is actually
happening.

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:08.822
I think something different is happening
with how companies are getting built.

00:05:08.822 --> 00:05:11.515
And maybe I can do the long version, kinda
the, the slightly long version of

00:05:11.515 --> 00:05:14.505
this, which I, I think there's actually a
whole new, a whole new way companies are

00:05:14.505 --> 00:05:17.451
being built in the last ten years and, and
I think that business people and MBAs

00:05:17.451 --> 00:05:20.912
turn out to be very central to it in a way
that's different than the past.

00:05:20.912 --> 00:05:22.064
So I kinda divide the story of

00:05:22.064 --> 00:05:24.464
how technology, the great technology
companies got built

00:05:24.464 --> 00:05:27.800
kind of in the three phases and I think
we're in the third phase now.

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:30.250
The first phase was in the 40s, 50s, 60s,
70s.

00:05:30.250 --> 00:05:32.380
And it was so crazily hard.

00:05:32.380 --> 00:05:33.990
If you talk to people who were in business
then or you

00:05:33.990 --> 00:05:36.140
read the stories, it was so hard to build
a new tech company.

00:05:36.140 --> 00:05:39.430
It was such an unbelievably, sort of
exceptional thing to do that you,

00:05:39.430 --> 00:05:42.030
you, you only really have these really
extreme characters who, who would do it.

00:05:42.030 --> 00:05:44.140
And there were a pretty small number of
them.

00:05:44.140 --> 00:05:46.537
And they were extreme, extreme characters,
like they were, they

00:05:46.537 --> 00:05:49.240
make all the current, like, high octane
entrepreneurs look like wusses.

00:05:50.868 --> 00:05:52.821
And the ones I'm thinking of, Thomas
Watson Senior.

00:05:52.821 --> 00:05:54.669
If you want to read, like, what it's like
to work

00:05:54.669 --> 00:05:57.770
for somebody who's harsh, read the book on
Thomas Watson Senior.

00:05:57.770 --> 00:05:59.350
You know, he makes, he makes all

00:05:59.350 --> 00:06:01.783
of today's entrepreneurs look like cream
puffs.

00:06:01.783 --> 00:06:02.134
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:06:02.134 --> 00:06:03.672
&gt;&gt; He would just literally sit in his
staff

00:06:03.672 --> 00:06:05.722
meetings for like five hours and just
scream at his,

00:06:05.722 --> 00:06:07.864
scream at his guys, there's just this,
then he built

00:06:07.864 --> 00:06:10.310
this astonishing company, IBM, off the
other side of that.

00:06:12.310 --> 00:06:13.582
David Packard.

00:06:13.582 --> 00:06:15.510
David Packard, actually, was quite a
character.

00:06:15.510 --> 00:06:18.610
He, David Packard, people now remember for
the HP way and for kind

00:06:18.610 --> 00:06:21.870
of that whole warm and fuzzy, you know,
kind of approach to running companies.

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:25.336
When, when David Packard was actually
running HP, he had two nicknames.

00:06:25.336 --> 00:06:26.919
One was Pappy, which is kind of what

00:06:26.919 --> 00:06:29.640
people remember in a kind of paternal
instinct, type.

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:31.120
His other nickname was the Mean One.

00:06:32.620 --> 00:06:35.570
And he similarly would just, you know,
tear people apart.

00:06:35.570 --> 00:06:37.350
And then Ross Perot is my favorite
example.

00:06:37.350 --> 00:06:39.340
Ross Perot built the first great
outsourcing company,

00:06:39.340 --> 00:06:41.490
one of the big tech successes in the 60s.

00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:43.910
And of course, you know, he was fantastic
as a business builder

00:06:43.910 --> 00:06:46.390
when he came into contact with the
American public, people went, what?

00:06:47.770 --> 00:06:49.710
and, you know, again this sort of extreme
personality.

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:51.580
So you get into this, this kind of, this

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:54.104
sort of will to power thing that was
happening.

00:06:54.104 --> 00:06:56.390
and, by the way, the VCs in those days, I
think, were very similar.

00:06:56.390 --> 00:06:59.287
Tom Perkins, who's become re-famous again
lately, you

00:06:59.287 --> 00:07:01.160
know is, is the same kind of character.

00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:02.780
He's, he's an ex-, he's a very, very

00:07:02.780 --> 00:07:04.680
extreme character and, and, and he always
was.

00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:06.466
But that's what it took, you know, for him
to do

00:07:06.466 --> 00:07:09.560
what he did in the 70s, and 80s in venture
capital.

00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:12.369
So those were kinda the extreme days and
then I think both VC and

00:07:12.369 --> 00:07:16.212
entrepreneurship, tech entrepreneurship,
sort of professionalized, and

00:07:16.212 --> 00:07:17.473
so you had a lot of VCs then.

00:07:17.473 --> 00:07:21.301
And this includes great VCs, John Doerr,
Mike Morris, Jim Breyer, you

00:07:21.301 --> 00:07:26.430
know, who are business people or investors
first, and, and never ran companies.

00:07:26.430 --> 00:07:30.030
And then you have this kind of move
through the 90s where you had this kind

00:07:30.030 --> 00:07:32.120
of default model where the one thing
everybody

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:33.950
knew was that founders couldn't possibly
run their companies.

00:07:33.950 --> 00:07:37.222
And so you would have a founder and then
you would basically promote or fire them

00:07:37.222 --> 00:07:41.340
to chairman or CTO and then you'd put in a
professional CEO as fast as possible.

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:43.936
And I think what happened is that model
just got extreme.

00:07:43.936 --> 00:07:46.584
And i think by the late 90s in the Valley,
we were mostly building

00:07:46.584 --> 00:07:49.985
companies that were kind of shells, or,
you know, kind of like puff pastries of

00:07:49.985 --> 00:07:51.973
companies where, you know, they didn't
really

00:07:51.973 --> 00:07:53.390
have, at the height of the bubble in

00:07:53.390 --> 00:07:57.690
'98, '99, the products that were getting
built for the most part weren't very good.

00:07:57.690 --> 00:08:00.597
And these companies were kind of on this
bomb run to get public as fast

00:08:00.597 --> 00:08:04.522
as possible, and you had all these catch
phrases, like go big or go home.

00:08:04.522 --> 00:08:08.358
Or my other favorite one at the time which
was, forget details, just do deals.

00:08:08.358 --> 00:08:09.769
And so you have this really kinda

00:08:09.769 --> 00:08:12.820
mercenary, hit and run approach to
building companies.

00:08:12.820 --> 00:08:14.966
And then all those companies vaporized
after the crash

00:08:14.966 --> 00:08:17.170
cuz it turned out they didn't have
valuable products.

00:08:17.170 --> 00:08:19.330
They didn't have deep engineering
capability.

00:08:19.330 --> 00:08:20.630
And then all the engineers who worked

00:08:20.630 --> 00:08:22.390
for those companies hated working for
those companies.

00:08:22.390 --> 00:08:24.751
Cuz they were completely sales-driven,
sales-led,

00:08:24.751 --> 00:08:26.920
these kind of mercenary kind of exercises.

00:08:26.920 --> 00:08:30.130
At, at the, at the height of, of, of how
bad it got.

00:08:30.130 --> 00:08:31.970
Now I think you've got the exact opposite
thing.

00:08:31.970 --> 00:08:35.006
I think the pendulum has swung all the way
in the other direction, which is, now

00:08:35.006 --> 00:08:36.863
we all understand and take for granted,
founder

00:08:36.863 --> 00:08:38.600
CEO, technical founder CEO is a good
thing.

00:08:38.600 --> 00:08:41.225
You know, Mark Zuckerberg is kinda the
apotheosis

00:08:41.225 --> 00:08:43.672
of kinda the, the idea that we have now.

00:08:43.672 --> 00:08:46.450
And so now what's been lost for a lot of
the entrepreneurs.

00:08:46.450 --> 00:08:48.400
A lot of the entrepreneurs are engineers,
but not business people.

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:51.554
Now what's been lost is a lot of the
actual art of building a business.

00:08:51.554 --> 00:08:54.806
and, in particular, what's been lost is
the art of sales and marketing.

00:08:54.806 --> 00:08:56.433
And a lot of today's founders, one of the

00:08:56.433 --> 00:08:58.490
big issues we deal with is they're very
technical.

00:08:58.490 --> 00:08:59.770
They're very product-centric.

00:08:59.770 --> 00:09:02.140
They're building great technology and they
just don't have a clue about sales and

00:09:02.140 --> 00:09:05.160
marketing, and what's more is they almost
have an aversion to learning about it.

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:07.652
It's almost like a post traumatic stress
kind of

00:09:07.652 --> 00:09:10.522
thing, you know, like 15 years after the
crash.

00:09:10.522 --> 00:09:11.839
And so now the challenge for a lot of

00:09:11.839 --> 00:09:14.068
these companies is how to take what are
actually fantastic

00:09:14.068 --> 00:09:16.980
products and fantastic technology and then
integrate in top-end business

00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:18.572
thinking, top-end sales and marketing

00:09:18.572 --> 00:09:20.970
thinking, and top-end operational
thinking.

00:09:20.970 --> 00:09:22.630
So I think we have actually collectively
have

00:09:22.630 --> 00:09:24.910
a huge opportunity to put the pieces back
together.

00:09:24.910 --> 00:09:26.917
And I think that's what the next five
years are going to be about.

00:09:26.917 --> 00:09:28.484
&gt;&gt; Could you see the role of MBAs in

00:09:28.484 --> 00:09:31.935
terms of helping scale through that sales
and marketing function?

00:09:31.935 --> 00:09:35.137
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so, yes, definitely and, and, in
fact, in the abstract,

00:09:35.137 --> 00:09:39.242
there is kind of two models, that are both
actually working quite well.

00:09:39.242 --> 00:09:42.860
The kind of reference model now is the
Mark Zuckerberg, Sheryl Sandberg model.

00:09:42.860 --> 00:09:45.220
And I work with Sheryl at Facebook and I
tease her all the time.

00:09:45.220 --> 00:09:47.030
She's lost control over her own name.

00:09:47.030 --> 00:09:48.496
It's now become a proper noun.

00:09:48.496 --> 00:09:49.028
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:09:49.028 --> 00:09:51.177
&gt;&gt; You know every 24-year-old technical
founder,

00:09:51.177 --> 00:09:52.712
you know, was like, I need a Sheryl.

00:09:52.712 --> 00:09:55.013
And I'm like, so do 400 other people.

00:09:55.013 --> 00:09:57.281
Unfortunately, human cloning is not quite
at the

00:09:57.281 --> 00:09:59.849
stage yet where we can fulfill everybody's
need.

00:09:59.849 --> 00:10:03.390
But basically the model of a very
high-powered business person with

00:10:03.390 --> 00:10:07.663
deep capabilities in sales, marketing, and
operations, who's able to partner

00:10:07.663 --> 00:10:09.982
as a number two, as a president or COO,
with a

00:10:09.982 --> 00:10:14.330
technical founder, CEO, when you have
somebody like a Mark Zuckerberg.

00:10:14.330 --> 00:10:16.104
So that's one model that works very well.

00:10:16.104 --> 00:10:18.873
And one of the interesting things about
the last five or ten years is more

00:10:18.873 --> 00:10:22.102
and more of the top end business leaders
in Silicon Valley have figured this out.

00:10:22.102 --> 00:10:25.126
And, like Sheryl, have chosen to partner
not as the CEO, but as

00:10:25.126 --> 00:10:26.359
the president or COO with a

00:10:26.359 --> 00:10:28.895
great technical founder and build great
companies.

00:10:28.895 --> 00:10:30.834
A recent example, Dennis Woodside, who's a

00:10:30.834 --> 00:10:33.321
top-end Google product or business
executive, just left

00:10:33.321 --> 00:10:37.087
and became number two at Dropbox to Drew
Houston, who's another one of these guys.

00:10:37.087 --> 00:10:39.031
And so that's one model and I think that's

00:10:39.031 --> 00:10:41.667
a very exciting model and I think it's
working well.

00:10:41.667 --> 00:10:43.266
The other model is what you might call

00:10:43.266 --> 00:10:45.531
sort of the Bill Campbell, Scott Cook
model.

00:10:45.531 --> 00:10:47.959
Or maybe the Dick Costolo, model as sort
of

00:10:47.959 --> 00:10:50.692
the other example, which is, in the case
where these

00:10:50.692 --> 00:10:53.732
companies don't have a founder who's
capable of being CEO

00:10:53.732 --> 00:10:56.037
or who wants to be CEO, to have a business

00:10:56.037 --> 00:10:58.950
person, become the CEO, but with the sort
of, with

00:10:58.950 --> 00:11:01.929
a much more advanced understanding of the
role of founders

00:11:01.929 --> 00:11:05.091
and the role of product strategy and
technology strategy than

00:11:05.091 --> 00:11:07.840
I think the professional CEOs got into in
the 90s.

00:11:07.840 --> 00:11:10.616
So, and, and this is the, I, I describe
this as the Bill Campbell-Scott

00:11:10.616 --> 00:11:14.374
Cook model because that's maybe the best
example in the history of the Valley.

00:11:14.374 --> 00:11:17.587
Which is, you know, Bill Campbell,
probably well known to the folks

00:11:17.587 --> 00:11:20.596
in the audience, you know, is not himself
a technologist or a product

00:11:20.596 --> 00:11:24.268
person, but is an outstanding operator of
businesses, has profoundly deep respect

00:11:24.268 --> 00:11:26.155
for founders, and has profoundly deep

00:11:26.155 --> 00:11:29.172
respect for products and, and for
technology.

00:11:29.172 --> 00:11:31.905
And always makes it a point in, in his
career, he's always made

00:11:31.905 --> 00:11:34.741
it a point to partner with the engineers
as opposed to be threatened

00:11:34.741 --> 00:11:37.274
by them or feel like, you know, they have
to be, you know,

00:11:37.274 --> 00:11:40.170
in the case of the technical founder, they
have to be forced out.

00:11:40.170 --> 00:11:43.186
And, of course, Apple, Apple over the
years has been a case study of

00:11:43.186 --> 00:11:46.056
this, and, of course, Bill came up for
Apple and so he saw this.

00:11:46.056 --> 00:11:48.008
And so you kind of contrast the now
legendary

00:11:48.008 --> 00:11:50.066
kind of John Scully-Steve Jobs model to to
the

00:11:50.066 --> 00:11:52.336
Bill Campbell-Scott Cook model and you
kind of see

00:11:52.336 --> 00:11:54.696
how, you know, kind of where that came
from.

00:11:54.696 --> 00:11:55.419
And so that's a model that can also work
very well.

00:11:55.419 --> 00:11:58.864
And so as, as the folks here think about
as you build your careers, and think about

00:11:58.864 --> 00:12:00.704
these things, I think if you're gonna be

00:12:00.704 --> 00:12:02.783
in the tech industry, the really key
question, you

00:12:02.783 --> 00:12:04.389
know, it might turn out either way, but

00:12:04.389 --> 00:12:06.611
the really key question is what's the
partnership that

00:12:06.611 --> 00:12:08.926
you're gonna have with the technical
visionary, in

00:12:08.926 --> 00:12:11.000
the company who will often be a technical
founder?

00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:14.901
And I think if you can crack that code, I
think there is

00:12:14.901 --> 00:12:20.740
just an enormous opportunity to, you know,
to have one plus one equals three.

00:12:20.740 --> 00:12:22.534
&gt;&gt; So at, at Andreessen Horowitz, the, the
VC fund

00:12:22.534 --> 00:12:24.682
you founded, you invest in many of these
founder COs.

00:12:24.682 --> 00:12:25.670
They all want a share-all?

00:12:25.670 --> 00:12:25.930
&gt;&gt; Yep.

00:12:25.930 --> 00:12:28.790
&gt;&gt; And finding a share-all isn't, isn't
necessarily that easy.

00:12:28.790 --> 00:12:33.277
And you've built up a, a very disruptive
model within the venture capital industry

00:12:33.277 --> 00:12:35.362
where you provide a lot of value-added

00:12:35.362 --> 00:12:39.440
services including hiring and marketing,
to portfolio companies.

00:12:39.440 --> 00:12:42.783
Could you talk a little about, how you
came up with that disruptive

00:12:42.783 --> 00:12:44.791
model and what opportunities you see going

00:12:44.791 --> 00:12:46.980
forward to continue shaping the VC
industry?

00:12:46.980 --> 00:12:49.114
&gt;&gt; Yes, so my partner and I came up as
entrepreneurs

00:12:49.114 --> 00:12:51.403
kind of in the phase where the assumption
was that you

00:12:51.403 --> 00:12:53.849
fire or demote the technical founder and
you bring in the

00:12:53.849 --> 00:12:57.200
professional CEO and you become a sales,
sort of a sales-driven company.

00:12:57.200 --> 00:13:00.211
And so, and we, we kind of, we have a lot
of experience with that model.

00:13:00.211 --> 00:13:02.398
Like I said, sometimes it works sometimes
it doesn't.

00:13:02.398 --> 00:13:05.835
But we thought, this is, we started our
firm, we planned our firm in

00:13:05.835 --> 00:13:10.544
2007-2008 and started it in '09 and our
basic take was there was an opportunity.

00:13:10.544 --> 00:13:14.548
Many of the other venture capital firms
had tilted hard in the direction of sort

00:13:14.548 --> 00:13:17.400
of professional sales-driven CEO, we
decided to tilt

00:13:17.400 --> 00:13:19.900
hard in the direction of technical founder
CEO.

00:13:19.900 --> 00:13:22.822
And so, we basically said, how would you
build a venture capital

00:13:22.822 --> 00:13:25.759
firm optimized for a technical founder who
wants to become a CEO?

00:13:26.776 --> 00:13:29.519
That let us, and by the way, not
religious, that's not the only thing we

00:13:29.519 --> 00:13:32.410
do, but like, how would you center the
culture of the firm around that idea?

00:13:32.410 --> 00:13:35.160
And I'll, I'll come back to the other
part.

00:13:35.160 --> 00:13:37.290
So we kinda decided on two things that
would come out of that.

00:13:37.290 --> 00:13:40.741
Which is, one is, if you have somebody
running a company who

00:13:40.741 --> 00:13:44.465
has not run a company before or has not,
maybe, necessarily been

00:13:44.465 --> 00:13:47.985
a manager in some cases before, or, in
some cases, maybe has

00:13:47.985 --> 00:13:51.936
not held a job before, they become CEO of
their own company.

00:13:51.936 --> 00:13:55.172
It really shines a very bright light on
the, the background, and caliber

00:13:55.172 --> 00:13:58.279
of the general partner that you're going
to propose to put on their board.

00:13:58.279 --> 00:14:00.163
And we just made the decision that, and
there

00:14:00.163 --> 00:14:02.297
are many different kinds of successful
VCs, but we

00:14:02.297 --> 00:14:04.281
just made the decision at our firm, the
general

00:14:04.281 --> 00:14:07.580
partners will be people who have built
technology startups before.

00:14:07.580 --> 00:14:10.450
And so I think at this point, seven of our
eight GPs,

00:14:10.450 --> 00:14:13.000
I think I'm the only one who hasn't
actually been the CEO.

00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:14.920
I think seven of the eight of our GPs have
been a, a

00:14:14.920 --> 00:14:19.216
CEO and I think five or six of the eight
now have been founders.

00:14:19.216 --> 00:14:21.914
And so, sort of by definition at our firm,
you get

00:14:21.914 --> 00:14:25.791
somebody on the board who really knows,
has been through the war.

00:14:25.791 --> 00:14:28.706
Really understands, you know, what things
are like.

00:14:28.706 --> 00:14:30.372
And so, when something goes wrong, and,
you

00:14:30.372 --> 00:14:32.848
know, things are just, like, horribly,
like, crashing.

00:14:32.848 --> 00:14:35.625
You know, the key engineer quits, or the
founders can't get along.

00:14:35.625 --> 00:14:37.011
Or the biggest customer dumps you.

00:14:37.011 --> 00:14:38.610
Or a competitor comes out with a much
better product.

00:14:38.610 --> 00:14:40.892
And all these really horrible things that
happen.

00:14:40.892 --> 00:14:43.868
You can't raise money, that we have
somebody in the board seat who is

00:14:43.868 --> 00:14:46.748
a really good advisor and can say, I was
in that situation before, and I

00:14:46.748 --> 00:14:49.772
can tell you what doesn't work, cuz I
probably made all those mistakes and

00:14:49.772 --> 00:14:52.660
then I can tell you, you know, gives you
some advice on what does work.

00:14:52.660 --> 00:14:55.846
So that's one, and then the other thing we
said was we said, okay, well

00:14:55.846 --> 00:14:59.399
what's, what's the, you know, we sort of
thought about what are the reasons why?

00:14:59.399 --> 00:15:01.134
You know, if, if a VC brings in a
professional

00:15:01.134 --> 00:15:02.836
CEO and fires the founder, why do they do
it?

00:15:02.836 --> 00:15:04.371
Part of it is likely experience.

00:15:04.371 --> 00:15:08.784
The other thing a professional CEO brings
in, is very, very deep network.

00:15:08.784 --> 00:15:11.923
If you've been a, you know, VP or general
manager,

00:15:11.923 --> 00:15:14.739
or CEO in Silica Valley for 20 years, you
have this

00:15:14.739 --> 00:15:18.391
enormous network of executives who you can
hire and engineers, and

00:15:18.391 --> 00:15:22.300
recruiters and you know all the reporters
and all the editors.

00:15:22.300 --> 00:15:25.410
And you know all of the customers.

00:15:25.410 --> 00:15:27.700
You know CIO's, and CTO's and you know how
to go sell the

00:15:27.700 --> 00:15:30.070
things and you know all the VC's you know
how to raise money.

00:15:30.070 --> 00:15:31.263
So you just have the business people in

00:15:31.263 --> 00:15:32.716
the valley, who have been in the valley,
have

00:15:32.716 --> 00:15:34.397
this just giant network of people and
these technical

00:15:34.397 --> 00:15:36.590
founders often don't because they've often
been heads down.

00:15:36.590 --> 00:15:39.302
You know, writing code most of their, most
of their lives.

00:15:39.302 --> 00:15:42.287
And so basically, what we decided was,
let's preconstruct the network

00:15:42.287 --> 00:15:45.081
that will basically, where we can take a
technical founder, inject

00:15:45.081 --> 00:15:47.296
it straight in the network, and sort of
give them super

00:15:47.296 --> 00:15:50.800
powers of a network that's comparable to
what, John Chambers might have.

00:15:50.800 --> 00:15:52.670
And so, and, and that's been a very big
effort on our part.

00:15:52.670 --> 00:15:54.560
We have about 60 full-time professionals
now,

00:15:54.560 --> 00:15:56.690
across five operating teams in the firm.

00:15:56.690 --> 00:15:58.820
That are not GPs, but are full time

00:15:58.820 --> 00:16:02.790
professionals organized around the
different areas of the network.

00:16:02.790 --> 00:16:05.250
So, sales, business development, corporate
development,

00:16:05.250 --> 00:16:08.240
marketing and PR, executive talent,
engineering talent.

00:16:08.240 --> 00:16:10.190
And so, as an example when it comes time
to find

00:16:10.190 --> 00:16:12.790
a sharer as a consequence, we, waiting
for, we are trying to

00:16:12.790 --> 00:16:16.540
build very deep relationships with all the
sharer of both genders, throughout

00:16:16.540 --> 00:16:18.690
the valley just a sort of a normal part of
sort of

00:16:18.690 --> 00:16:21.940
art network building exercise, and then
when we have companies that are

00:16:21.940 --> 00:16:24.640
kind of maturing to the point when they
need somebody like that,

00:16:24.640 --> 00:16:27.140
you know, as one example, we will know who
those people are

00:16:27.140 --> 00:16:30.430
and we'll have kind of you know, very easy
access to them.

00:16:30.430 --> 00:16:32.130
You kind of help them to sort of bridge
the gap.

00:16:32.130 --> 00:16:34.843
&gt;&gt; No that's, that's very understand,
helpful understanding

00:16:34.843 --> 00:16:37.640
how you lean in once you've identified the
founders.

00:16:37.640 --> 00:16:39.340
&gt;&gt; That was good, that was good.

00:16:39.340 --> 00:16:39.599
&gt;&gt; Stop it.

00:16:39.599 --> 00:16:40.219
&gt;&gt; That was good.

00:16:40.219 --> 00:16:42.170
&gt;&gt; The how do you actually identify them
initially?

00:16:42.170 --> 00:16:47.423
And so what do you think are the, the
traits that founders have and I'd

00:16:47.423 --> 00:16:52.870
also love to hear about some of the best
and worst pitches you ever heard.

00:16:54.630 --> 00:16:56.896
&gt;&gt; So the, the basic math is the, so
there's

00:16:56.896 --> 00:17:00.140
a basic math component and then there's
the, all the intangible.

00:17:00.140 --> 00:17:03.740
So the basic math component is there's
about 4,000 start-ups a year that

00:17:03.740 --> 00:17:08.150
are founded in the technology industry
that would like to raise venture capital.

00:17:08.150 --> 00:17:09.740
We can invest in about 20.

00:17:09.740 --> 00:17:11.990
So the falloff is significant.

00:17:11.990 --> 00:17:15.188
we, I like to say our day job is crushing
entrepreneurs' hopes and dreams.

00:17:15.188 --> 00:17:18.500
We actually have focused very, very hard
on being very

00:17:18.500 --> 00:17:22.050
good at saying no cuz that's mostly what
we do.

00:17:22.050 --> 00:17:26.110
We see actually, we see 3,000 inbound
referred opportunities a year.

00:17:26.110 --> 00:17:31.430
We narrow that down to a couple hundred
that are taken particularly seriously.

00:17:31.430 --> 00:17:32.660
And I would say there's kind of this very

00:17:32.660 --> 00:17:35.510
interesting kind of process where there's
you know, say the

00:17:35.510 --> 00:17:36.900
hard thing is deciding which one's we're
going to

00:17:36.900 --> 00:17:39.586
invest in, because we can just invest in
so few.

00:17:39.586 --> 00:17:42.183
The somewhat easier thing actually it
turns out, this has been

00:17:42.183 --> 00:17:44.486
a surprise, it's actually after you have
been in it for

00:17:44.486 --> 00:17:46.985
a while, the thing that's actually fairly
easy to tell is,

00:17:46.985 --> 00:17:49.110
will this team and company be fundable by
a top VC.

00:17:49.110 --> 00:17:51.060
Will it get funded by a top VC.

00:17:51.060 --> 00:17:53.670
It may be, it may be Sequoia, or Excel, or
Greylock, or who, who

00:17:53.670 --> 00:17:57.150
knows who it is, but you know, does this
company kind of clear the bar?

00:17:57.150 --> 00:18:00.010
And I think the way the math works,
basically, is, you

00:18:00.010 --> 00:18:03.270
know, there's about 200 a year that are
fundable by top VCs.

00:18:03.270 --> 00:18:05.016
That, that, that get funded.

00:18:05.016 --> 00:18:06.693
By the way, within the 200, about 15 of
those

00:18:06.693 --> 00:18:08.930
will generate, you know, 95% plus of all
the economic return.

00:18:09.932 --> 00:18:13.138
So just cuz it gets funded by a top VC
doesn't mean, it,

00:18:13.138 --> 00:18:17.798
even the top VCs right tank, you know,
generally, about half their deals.

00:18:17.798 --> 00:18:21.010
So even if you get funded by a top VC,
it's not complete validation.

00:18:21.010 --> 00:18:22.780
So about 200 a year that are kinda
fundable by top VCs.

00:18:22.780 --> 00:18:26.080
We can fund 20 and then 15 of them
actually generate all the returns.

00:18:26.080 --> 00:18:28.290
And so, it's kind of a white knuckle thing
when it gets

00:18:28.290 --> 00:18:30.200
right down to it, to try to make, you
know, the picks.

00:18:30.200 --> 00:18:33.691
And if there's one thing that's
frustrating in this job, that every VC

00:18:33.691 --> 00:18:37.280
deals with it's, you know, you miss most
of the big winners, right?

00:18:37.280 --> 00:18:40.820
It's like, the thing all the top ventures
have in common is they did not

00:18:40.820 --> 00:18:42.708
invest in most of the great successful

00:18:42.708 --> 00:18:46.990
technology companies, which is an
incredibly frustrating thing.

00:18:46.990 --> 00:18:50.258
So, that's the basic dynamic, and that's
the framework within

00:18:50.258 --> 00:18:52.932
which, you know, people come in and pitch
to us.

00:18:52.932 --> 00:18:56.570
At the heart of it, there's two things
which we look really, really hard for.

00:18:56.570 --> 00:18:59.000
I mean, there's the kind of surface level
stuff you look for.

00:18:59.000 --> 00:19:01.670
So if you look for a huge market, you look
for, you know, differentiating technology.

00:19:01.670 --> 00:19:03.434
And you look for you know, incredible
people.

00:19:03.434 --> 00:19:05.018
I think in practice, I think

00:19:05.018 --> 00:19:08.054
that we collectively and certainly, we
specifically

00:19:08.054 --> 00:19:12.080
and then we collectively, VCs, I think we
probably, we, we spend a lot

00:19:12.080 --> 00:19:14.588
of time talking about markets and
technology

00:19:14.588 --> 00:19:16.304
and we have lots of opinions and

00:19:16.304 --> 00:19:18.350
I'm not sure that those opinions are

00:19:18.350 --> 00:19:21.010
actually all that relevant, all that
often.

00:19:21.010 --> 00:19:25.308
I think probably, the decision ultimately,
is and should be around people.

00:19:25.308 --> 00:19:28.200
As like 90% of the decision.

00:19:28.200 --> 00:19:31.650
The two things we really zero in on on
people are, you know, two things.

00:19:31.650 --> 00:19:34.950
They sound simple and they end up being
very difficult: courage and genius.

00:19:36.320 --> 00:19:37.420
Courage is the one we talk about a

00:19:37.420 --> 00:19:40.120
lot because it's the one that people can
learn.

00:19:40.120 --> 00:19:44.194
You know, courage which is to say not
giving up in the face of adversity.

00:19:44.194 --> 00:19:46.663
You know, just being absolutely determined
to succeed, you know, is

00:19:46.663 --> 00:19:48.900
something that, you can, you can like,
force yourself to do.

00:19:48.900 --> 00:19:51.280
It can be very painful, you can force
yourself to do it.

00:19:51.280 --> 00:19:54.118
The genius part is a little bit hard to
force yourself to do.

00:19:54.118 --> 00:19:56.501
You know, courage without genius might not
get you where

00:19:56.501 --> 00:19:59.530
you need to go but genius without courage
almost certainly won't.

00:19:59.530 --> 00:20:03.670
And so, we're looking for some kind of
magic combination of genius and courage.

00:20:03.670 --> 00:20:06.178
You know, there's there's one of my
partners

00:20:06.178 --> 00:20:08.110
quotes, he quotes Nichie a lot on these,
he

00:20:08.110 --> 00:20:10.665
says it's, it's will to power, it's, it's

00:20:10.665 --> 00:20:12.890
you know, it's people who simply will not
stop.

00:20:13.924 --> 00:20:16.547
and, and by the way, right, there's always
been this kind of thing at

00:20:16.547 --> 00:20:19.820
Silicon Valley of like, sort of this, like
I call it the failure fetish, right?

00:20:19.820 --> 00:20:20.800
Failure is good, right?

00:20:20.800 --> 00:20:23.430
Failure, you guys have probably all been
taught this.

00:20:23.430 --> 00:20:25.370
Heard about this from a lot of people like
failure is a wonderful

00:20:25.370 --> 00:20:28.110
thing, failure teaches you all this stuff,
and it's great to fail a lot.

00:20:28.110 --> 00:20:30.190
Like, and we don't like buy any of that.

00:20:30.190 --> 00:20:32.750
We think that's all complete, complete
nonsense.

00:20:32.750 --> 00:20:34.324
We think failure sucks.

00:20:34.324 --> 00:20:39.290
[LAUGH] We think failure is a terribly,
terribly depressing thing to go through.

00:20:39.290 --> 00:20:40.870
We think success, on the other hand, is
wonderful.

00:20:42.160 --> 00:20:44.053
You know, you wouldn't think that this is

00:20:44.053 --> 00:20:46.060
something you have to actually say out
loud.

00:20:46.060 --> 00:20:49.602
But we, we do find it to be clarifying
when we point it out.

00:20:49.602 --> 00:20:51.470
And so we are strongly biased towards
people

00:20:51.470 --> 00:20:53.238
who are so determined to succeed that they

00:20:53.238 --> 00:20:57.060
just, they never give up, they never quit,
and I think that's a huge part of it.

00:20:57.060 --> 00:21:00.596
And that's something we really look deep,
we look incredibly deeply for, you

00:21:00.596 --> 00:21:03.740
know that's the kind of thing that's not
listed on a resume right.

00:21:03.740 --> 00:21:05.960
That's something that has to be deep in
somebody's, fundamentally deep

00:21:05.960 --> 00:21:08.960
in somebody's character and you have to
see it in their backgrounds.

00:21:08.960 --> 00:21:11.628
&gt;&gt; And what has been your, talking of
courage, what has been

00:21:11.628 --> 00:21:15.450
your most courageous moment, and perhaps
the moment of which you're most proud?

00:21:15.450 --> 00:21:17.361
&gt;&gt; Oh, the moment, actually this is
actually a good day to

00:21:17.361 --> 00:21:20.176
ask that question because my partner Ben's
book actually came out today.

00:21:20.176 --> 00:21:24.788
So if you haven't bought it yet, number
one on Kindle in management, $14.44.

00:21:24.788 --> 00:21:26.087
$14.44.

00:21:26.087 --> 00:21:32.351
[LAUGH] Makes a, makes a great birthday
present for all of your friends.

00:21:32.351 --> 00:21:34.513
[LAUGH] He actually tells the story in his
book,

00:21:34.513 --> 00:21:36.629
it's actually in his book, and it's it's
when our,

00:21:36.629 --> 00:21:38.791
it's actually it's it's when our, it's
it's, our

00:21:38.791 --> 00:21:41.840
second company, Loud Cloud, when, when got
just taken apart.

00:21:41.840 --> 00:21:44.600
We started our second company Loud Cloud
in September 1999.

00:21:44.600 --> 00:21:47.570
And it was classic, you know, we were, I
mean it was fantastic.

00:21:47.570 --> 00:21:50.550
It was incredibly high rapid growth rate
off of a standing start.

00:21:50.550 --> 00:21:53.910
Straight into the, you know, the the last
six months of the bubble.

00:21:53.910 --> 00:21:55.060
You know, unprecedented growth.

00:21:55.060 --> 00:21:56.000
Cover of Wired magazine.

00:21:56.000 --> 00:21:56.540
On and on and on.

00:21:56.540 --> 00:21:58.255
We took it public in 18 months, and then

00:21:58.255 --> 00:22:01.370
just the world caved in, our entire
business caved in.

00:22:01.370 --> 00:22:03.700
burning, you know, an enormous amount of
cash, cuz we

00:22:03.700 --> 00:22:08.460
were, we had, created a company for much,
much higher growth.

00:22:08.460 --> 00:22:11.416
And and our stock, ultimately bottomed out
at half of cash.

00:22:11.416 --> 00:22:15.959
So our shareholders, made the judgement,
that not only were we so incompetent,

00:22:15.959 --> 00:22:19.145
that we were not capable of justifying the
amount of cash we had in

00:22:19.145 --> 00:22:21.977
the bank, but that we were certain to burn
at least half of

00:22:21.977 --> 00:22:25.240
it before we would call it quits and just
give them the cash back.

00:22:25.240 --> 00:22:27.326
Those were probably the dark days.

00:22:27.326 --> 00:22:30.275
And then, you know, NASDAQ, you know,
start sending the D-listing letters

00:22:30.275 --> 00:22:32.607
and they send about one a day saying if
you don't get your,

00:22:32.607 --> 00:22:35.115
your, your stock back above a dollar,
we're gonna D-list you and

00:22:35.115 --> 00:22:37.832
you'll be on the pink sheets and we came
within days from that.

00:22:37.832 --> 00:22:39.774
And so guiding through that, and by the
way, guiding

00:22:39.774 --> 00:22:41.372
through that kind of thing at the same
time it

00:22:41.372 --> 00:22:43.271
looks like the entire world is ending,
that it looks

00:22:43.271 --> 00:22:46.332
like you know the tech industry will never
ever recover.

00:22:46.332 --> 00:22:48.594
I think we, the thing we are most proud

00:22:48.594 --> 00:22:51.860
of is, is, is actually working our way
through that.

00:22:51.860 --> 00:22:52.959
&gt;&gt; Fair enough that is a tough thing to
get in.

00:22:52.959 --> 00:22:58.384
The You spoke about Ben's, Ben's book and,
and Ben talks a

00:22:58.384 --> 00:23:02.045
lot about these, these challenges, the
whole notion in the book is

00:23:02.045 --> 00:23:05.451
dealing with the hard things and have
there been hard moments in

00:23:05.451 --> 00:23:09.460
your relationship and can you talk a
little bit about that relationship?

00:23:09.460 --> 00:23:13.185
I remember once reading that, he described
you as the Beyoncé of the

00:23:13.185 --> 00:23:17.540
relationship and him as Kelly Rowland, so
how do you feel about being Beyoncé?

00:23:17.540 --> 00:23:18.630
&gt;&gt; Yes, yes.

00:23:18.630 --> 00:23:20.643
I'm hoping, I'm hoping that wasn't a
commentary of

00:23:20.643 --> 00:23:22.716
my figure, that's my, that's my main,
that's my.

00:23:22.716 --> 00:23:23.296
[CROSSTALK].

00:23:23.296 --> 00:23:24.840
&gt;&gt; Or your dances.

00:23:24.840 --> 00:23:25.460
&gt;&gt; Or my dance moves.

00:23:26.600 --> 00:23:28.940
So, I would, let me, maybe brought it up,

00:23:28.940 --> 00:23:31.930
so it goes to the nature of business
partnership.

00:23:31.930 --> 00:23:34.680
So Ben and I have been partners for 18
years, so I first met Ben in 1995.

00:23:34.680 --> 00:23:36.633
He actually tells the story, in the book,
of how

00:23:36.633 --> 00:23:38.550
we met, which is a whole story in and of
itself.

00:23:38.550 --> 00:23:39.800
It involves a lot of curse words.

00:23:40.818 --> 00:23:43.169
You know, we kind of describe ours, I mean
we, we, we love

00:23:43.169 --> 00:23:46.670
each other and we, we, we do everything
together, everything in business together.

00:23:46.670 --> 00:23:50.020
We you know we describe ourselves a little
bit as the old married couple.

00:23:50.020 --> 00:23:52.690
Yeah, you know that, you see like out on
the park bench in the park in

00:23:52.690 --> 00:23:55.560
the middle of the afternoon, sitting on
opposite

00:23:55.560 --> 00:23:57.830
ends of the bench kinda staring at each
other.

00:23:57.830 --> 00:23:59.440
They're always there, but they're not
talking, you

00:23:59.440 --> 00:24:01.280
know, and maybe they argue every now and
then.

00:24:01.280 --> 00:24:02.950
But they're there this year and they'll be
there

00:24:02.950 --> 00:24:04.288
in five years, they'll be there in ten
years.

00:24:04.288 --> 00:24:07.782
So you, you know I would say at this
point, at this point it's troubly hard

00:24:07.782 --> 00:24:09.922
to untangle how the partnership works
other than

00:24:09.922 --> 00:24:12.248
just we've been working together for so
long.

00:24:12.248 --> 00:24:14.663
And so we have the we have a deep level of
trust that

00:24:14.663 --> 00:24:18.371
comes from understanding each other very
deeply, and so I know exactly what he's

00:24:18.371 --> 00:24:20.624
good at and I know what to defer to him on
and I know,

00:24:20.624 --> 00:24:24.270
he knows exactly what I'm good at and what
to defer to me on.

00:24:24.270 --> 00:24:27.760
And then both of us trust the other, you
know.

00:24:27.760 --> 00:24:30.353
So we, we both know that we'll make
decisions in both of our best

00:24:30.353 --> 00:24:32.709
interests, and there's never anything
that's you

00:24:32.709 --> 00:24:34.486
know, advantages one of us over the other,

00:24:34.486 --> 00:24:38.089
and so, as a consequence, each of us are
very comfortable, you know, essentially

00:24:38.089 --> 00:24:41.990
caving to the other on any topic, which I
think is, is actually very helpful.

00:24:41.990 --> 00:24:45.530
That said we argue about everything and we
constantly argue.

00:24:45.530 --> 00:24:48.920
and, you know, we often come at things
from very different points of

00:24:48.920 --> 00:24:52.950
view and, you know, in you know, I don't
even know how to describe.

00:24:52.950 --> 00:24:56.060
We, we just have different backgrounds
from before we have

00:24:56.060 --> 00:24:59.770
kind of different reference points for how
we think about things.

00:24:59.770 --> 00:25:03.950
He is a far better operator than I am, so
he's much better at running a business.

00:25:03.950 --> 00:25:07.958
So for example, a lot of things, when we,
when we used to run companies together.

00:25:07.958 --> 00:25:10.073
A big thing we'd argue about is, you know,
I would,

00:25:10.073 --> 00:25:11.812
I, I, sort of I think what he would say
about

00:25:11.812 --> 00:25:13.739
me is that I'm sort of abstract, so I
think about

00:25:13.739 --> 00:25:16.420
things like products and strategy and
business in an abstract way.

00:25:16.420 --> 00:25:18.090
And so for me it's like, okay, what's the
right answer?

00:25:18.090 --> 00:25:21.118
Like, what, what's the, you know, what
should we do?

00:25:21.118 --> 00:25:24.300
And then the way he thinks about it is,
from an organizational standpoint,

00:25:24.300 --> 00:25:27.732
from a management standpoint, is, what I,
what are we capable of doing?

00:25:27.732 --> 00:25:30.830
And so I will often propose things that,
where he's like, you're out of your mind.

00:25:30.830 --> 00:25:32.995
Like, the entire, you know, yes, in theory
that might be a

00:25:32.995 --> 00:25:36.370
good idea, in practice, you'll destroy the
entire company if we try that.

00:25:36.370 --> 00:25:38.572
And I'm like well, that's a pretty good
point.

00:25:38.572 --> 00:25:40.903
And then, you know the argument in the
other way is you know,

00:25:40.903 --> 00:25:43.648
look, I know that the organization is
gonna get challenged by this and I

00:25:43.648 --> 00:25:46.439
know it's gonna be hard and we might lose
people, but it's so important

00:25:46.439 --> 00:25:49.376
that we have to do this thing, that we
have to really push it.

00:25:49.376 --> 00:25:51.646
And so I think a lot of the theories that
he and I have

00:25:51.646 --> 00:25:54.782
developed over the years about how to run
companies are kind of at that

00:25:54.782 --> 00:25:57.273
intersection point of what's kind of
intellectually

00:25:57.273 --> 00:25:58.571
the correct thing to do or the

00:25:58.571 --> 00:26:03.060
optimal thing to do and then the actual
practical reality, of what can be done.

00:26:03.060 --> 00:26:04.570
He talks a lot about one of his theories
that

00:26:04.570 --> 00:26:07.290
we use a lot at the firm is uses his book

00:26:07.290 --> 00:26:09.100
is kind of all about this is we, we call

00:26:09.100 --> 00:26:12.510
it, there are no silver bullets, there are
only lead bullets.

00:26:12.510 --> 00:26:15.370
There's this, there's this temptation
especially when you get into crisis.

00:26:15.370 --> 00:26:17.010
When you get into real, real, real
problem, there's

00:26:17.010 --> 00:26:18.830
this temptation to think there must be a
magic answer.

00:26:18.830 --> 00:26:21.350
Like, there must be some stroke of genius,
you know.

00:26:21.350 --> 00:26:23.300
It's almost, it's like what you, I don't
know.

00:26:23.300 --> 00:26:26.140
if people watch the new Sherlock Holmes,
you know, TV series, which I just love.

00:26:26.140 --> 00:26:27.769
It's like, you know, Sherlock is gonna
have, you

00:26:27.769 --> 00:26:28.953
know, no matter how dire it gets and no

00:26:28.953 --> 00:26:30.768
matter how like evil Moriarty is,
Sherlock's gonna have

00:26:30.768 --> 00:26:32.340
that stroke of genius that's gonna save
the day.

00:26:32.340 --> 00:26:33.385
And there's this really strong tendency to
kind

00:26:33.385 --> 00:26:34.620
of think, that that, that that's out
there.

00:26:34.620 --> 00:26:38.460
And we see a lot of entrepreneurs that
kind of cycle through different silver

00:26:38.460 --> 00:26:41.760
bullets, and then they don't work and they
don't work and they don't work.

00:26:43.150 --> 00:26:44.760
Ben's point is always, it's probably the
answer,

00:26:44.760 --> 00:26:46.640
it's probably firing a whole bunch of lead
bullets.

00:26:46.640 --> 00:26:48.550
And so the answer probably is, you know,

00:26:48.550 --> 00:26:50.110
the engineers working, you know, later at
night

00:26:50.110 --> 00:26:51.940
for, you know, six months and, you know,

00:26:51.940 --> 00:26:53.690
getting the next version of the product
out.

00:26:53.690 --> 00:26:55.530
And the answer is probably for, you know,
the

00:26:55.530 --> 00:26:57.530
sales reps to go call in twice as many
customers

00:26:57.530 --> 00:26:58.960
and try to close some more deals and the
answer

00:26:58.960 --> 00:27:00.900
is probably to, you know, your stock price
is low.

00:27:00.900 --> 00:27:03.595
You know, go find the investors who are
willing to invest when you're

00:27:03.595 --> 00:27:06.400
trading at half the cash cuz it turns out
they actually do exist.

00:27:06.400 --> 00:27:08.190
And then your stock goes up a little bit.

00:27:08.190 --> 00:27:09.620
People start to regain confidence.

00:27:09.620 --> 00:27:10.660
And so, I would say, I've, you know, I've

00:27:10.660 --> 00:27:12.662
certainly come around to that point of
view a lot.

00:27:12.662 --> 00:27:14.726
And so whenever we work with entrepreneurs
we often

00:27:14.726 --> 00:27:16.886
have very similar advice cuz it's kind of,
it'll be

00:27:16.886 --> 00:27:19.238
tempered through the very practical
realities of what you

00:27:19.238 --> 00:27:21.170
have to do to get through a situation like
that.

00:27:22.850 --> 00:27:25.120
&gt;&gt; At yeah, that, that seems a, a very
healthy argument.

00:27:25.120 --> 00:27:27.500
Perhaps I can bring you on to a, an

00:27:27.500 --> 00:27:31.320
argument that, that's probably much harder
to deal with.

00:27:31.320 --> 00:27:37.310
I mean, in general you enjoy an incredibly
strong public image.

00:27:37.310 --> 00:27:40.445
And, last week, Carl Icahn, rather than
using either silver

00:27:40.445 --> 00:27:43.258
or lead bullet, used the kind of badly
trained Gatling gun.

00:27:43.258 --> 00:27:46.591
[LAUGH] And when, he a, he brought up, as
an investor of

00:27:46.591 --> 00:27:50.866
eBay and that eBay should divest PayPal
and allege the you had

00:27:50.866 --> 00:27:54.487
a conflict of interest and could you speak
a little bit about

00:27:54.487 --> 00:27:58.850
how it feels dealing with those sorts of
allegations in the press.

00:27:58.850 --> 00:28:01.480
&gt;&gt; I think that the and by the way it's
not just, it's

00:28:01.480 --> 00:28:05.930
you know, Carl has become very active in a
bunch of Tech companies lately.

00:28:05.930 --> 00:28:09.784
It's actually not just Carl, there's a
firm called Elliot Associates, it's

00:28:09.784 --> 00:28:13.890
a top hedge fund that's become very active
buying beaten down tech companies.

00:28:13.890 --> 00:28:15.158
There's a bunch of others.

00:28:15.158 --> 00:28:17.450
Actually this, this is part of my theory
of we're

00:28:17.450 --> 00:28:20.290
not in the bubble, which is when activists
become interested

00:28:20.290 --> 00:28:22.583
in a sector it's because the PEs are low,
because

00:28:22.583 --> 00:28:25.210
the cash balance is high and the debt
levels are low.

00:28:25.210 --> 00:28:27.380
And so, it, it, you know, although I'm
not, not, I'm not getting

00:28:27.380 --> 00:28:30.820
that much of a thrill out of the, my
current level of personal engagement.

00:28:30.820 --> 00:28:33.320
This stuff is, I view this, all this

00:28:33.320 --> 00:28:35.690
activist, and, activity as validation of,
of my

00:28:35.690 --> 00:28:39.390
thesis, which is, we're not only not in a
bubble, we're actually still in a bust.

00:28:39.390 --> 00:28:41.606
Especially the big tech companies are
still in a bust.

00:28:41.606 --> 00:28:45.290
Multiples are very low, cash balances are
very high.

00:28:45.290 --> 00:28:48.190
It's the kind of thing where I think time
will cure that.

00:28:48.190 --> 00:28:49.980
Because in time, PEs will expand.

00:28:49.980 --> 00:28:54.297
In time companies will invest more of
their cash in their own business or find

00:28:54.297 --> 00:28:56.264
other things to do with it and the

00:28:56.264 --> 00:28:59.230
activist will go back to harassing steel
mills.

00:28:59.230 --> 00:28:59.810
&gt;&gt; Yep.

00:28:59.810 --> 00:29:00.150
Fair enough.

00:29:00.150 --> 00:29:01.520
&gt;&gt; And oil companies and airlines.

00:29:01.520 --> 00:29:06.672
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it was, it was interesting to see
that in, as you, as you blogged in 2011,

00:29:06.672 --> 00:29:09.637
he was advocating exactly the, the sorts
of board

00:29:09.637 --> 00:29:12.900
management that he's now seems to be
criticizing it.

00:29:12.900 --> 00:29:16.434
&gt;&gt; Yeah, Carl Ichan in 2011 was extremely
enthusiastic about

00:29:16.434 --> 00:29:20.734
board nominees with conflicts of interest
that came from his organization.

00:29:20.734 --> 00:29:22.105
And so I, I posted from this morning

00:29:22.105 --> 00:29:24.600
extensive exerts from his communications
from that time.

00:29:24.600 --> 00:29:28.255
And so, as far as I'm concerned he can now
argue with himself.

00:29:28.255 --> 00:29:31.139
[LAUGH].

00:29:31.139 --> 00:29:33.760
&gt;&gt; And it's very helpful for us as, as

00:29:33.760 --> 00:29:37.476
students to understand how you actually
spend your day.

00:29:37.476 --> 00:29:41.136
And, and I'm sure it's a, an incredibly
busy one.

00:29:41.136 --> 00:29:42.270
So, thank you for taking the, the time out today.

00:29:42.270 --> 00:29:45.981
But could you perhaps describe what
yesterday looked like?

00:29:45.981 --> 00:29:47.735
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Oh, yesterday?

00:29:47.735 --> 00:29:49.510
Good lord, what did yesterday?

00:29:49.510 --> 00:29:50.560
What day was yesterday?

00:29:50.560 --> 00:29:50.760
&gt;&gt; Monday.

00:29:50.760 --> 00:29:50.940
&gt;&gt; Monday.

00:29:50.940 --> 00:29:52.183
Okay, good.

00:29:52.183 --> 00:29:54.409
So Monday is butts in seats day for us.

00:29:54.409 --> 00:29:56.333
So Monday is all day partner meeting.

00:29:56.333 --> 00:29:58.297
And all the VC firms kinda have this in
common.

00:29:58.297 --> 00:29:59.901
And so, like one of the really critic,
when you're starting a

00:29:59.901 --> 00:30:01.083
company, there's all these really critical

00:30:01.083 --> 00:30:02.368
issues, like what product you're gonna
build.

00:30:02.368 --> 00:30:04.003
[UNKNOWN] are going to go into.

00:30:04.003 --> 00:30:06.918
And he start a VC from what's really
important and what your

00:30:06.918 --> 00:30:10.840
conference table gonna look like, and how
comfortable your chair is gonna be.

00:30:10.840 --> 00:30:13.332
Because you are gonna be in those chairs
for along time.

00:30:13.332 --> 00:30:17.403
So it's basically, it's, you know,
sometimes, yesterday was like eight hours,

00:30:17.403 --> 00:30:20.660
it can be as long as 12 hours straight, of
just straight meetings.

00:30:20.660 --> 00:30:23.280
And so, it's basically two things happened
on a Monday.

00:30:23.280 --> 00:30:24.510
Well three things happened on Monday.

00:30:24.510 --> 00:30:26.340
So we have a, breakfast every Monday.

00:30:26.340 --> 00:30:28.100
Then we alternate breakfasts.

00:30:28.100 --> 00:30:29.907
We have a general partner breakfast, and
then

00:30:29.907 --> 00:30:31.761
we have a general partner plus senior
operating

00:30:31.761 --> 00:30:33.892
staff breakfast and we alternate back and
forth,

00:30:33.892 --> 00:30:35.923
and we kinda do all the firm related
things.

00:30:35.923 --> 00:30:40.402
And then we have back to back, what we
call the all GP pitch meetings.

00:30:40.402 --> 00:30:43.376
And so these are the companies that we are
most likely to invest in.

00:30:43.376 --> 00:30:45.371
And so the signal if you're raising
pension money,

00:30:45.371 --> 00:30:47.332
if you get invited on Monday, that's a
good sign.

00:30:47.332 --> 00:30:49.634
If you don't get invited on Monday it's
not a good sign.

00:30:49.634 --> 00:30:54.140
And so, and we have, and I think yesterday
we had three of those back to back.

00:30:54.140 --> 00:30:56.176
Sometimes we have four or even five.

00:30:56.176 --> 00:30:58.803
also, by the way, pitch early in the day
not late in the day.

00:30:58.803 --> 00:31:00.729
Just helpful advice.

00:31:00.729 --> 00:31:04.560
so, we do those, and then and then we
have, at

00:31:04.560 --> 00:31:08.660
the end of the day we have what we call
[UNKNOWN] review.

00:31:08.660 --> 00:31:10.060
so, we actually do that twice a week.

00:31:10.060 --> 00:31:11.302
Most firms do that once a week, but we
wanna

00:31:11.302 --> 00:31:13.090
move a little faster, so we do it twice a
week.

00:31:13.090 --> 00:31:16.415
Mondays and Thursdays, and so that's
basically a complete, you know,

00:31:16.415 --> 00:31:18.289
sort of, you know, basically a

00:31:18.289 --> 00:31:20.990
complete pipeline report, maybe possibly
interesting.

00:31:20.990 --> 00:31:22.464
So, so we actually run our deal process

00:31:22.464 --> 00:31:24.520
like a sale, like a, we actually use
salesfirst.com

00:31:24.520 --> 00:31:26.218
and we actually run our entire process and
the

00:31:26.218 --> 00:31:28.480
firm like a sales first runs a sales
pipeline.

00:31:28.480 --> 00:31:32.650
And so we have comprehensive tracking of
kind of the stage of every.

00:31:32.650 --> 00:31:34.410
All the way from all initial inbound
deals, all

00:31:34.410 --> 00:31:36.390
the way through the ones that are being
closed.

00:31:36.390 --> 00:31:39.180
And so we kind of review that entire
pipeline, and we

00:31:39.180 --> 00:31:42.030
actually have a team that you know, a team
that manages that.

00:31:42.030 --> 00:31:45.890
And a great, a great operating person who
who manages all that.

00:31:45.890 --> 00:31:47.270
And so we, we go through all that.

00:31:47.270 --> 00:31:48.060
And that's where we have.

00:31:48.060 --> 00:31:49.686
That's basically a long argument and we go

00:31:49.686 --> 00:31:51.570
through it and we argue about every
company.

00:31:51.570 --> 00:31:55.000
And we argue about everything else we can
think to argue.

00:31:55.000 --> 00:31:57.130
And then we go home and collapse.

00:31:57.130 --> 00:31:59.930
&gt;&gt; In those in those deals for the
meetings, are there any technologies?

00:31:59.930 --> 00:32:04.840
I know, for example, you've blogged
recently about Bit Coin and how

00:32:04.840 --> 00:32:07.180
excited you are about Bit Coin and the
future of the news industry.

00:32:07.180 --> 00:32:08.810
Are there any particular technologies or

00:32:08.810 --> 00:32:12.450
industries that you're, you're most,
excited about?

00:32:12.450 --> 00:32:15.370
&gt;&gt; So there's a two part, two part answer
to that.

00:32:15.370 --> 00:32:19.080
And so, the first answer I will answer the
question in the second part, but the,

00:32:19.080 --> 00:32:21.960
the, the first answer is, we, this is

00:32:21.960 --> 00:32:23.220
another kind of theory we have at the
firm.

00:32:23.220 --> 00:32:25.590
So, there are venture capital firms that
are

00:32:25.590 --> 00:32:27.850
very top down and thinking about markets
and technologies.

00:32:27.850 --> 00:32:32.072
So, if you go inside a particular sequoia
and Excel, and

00:32:32.072 --> 00:32:37.486
Bessemer, and I think Kleiner Perkins used
to, and may still.

00:32:37.486 --> 00:32:39.298
The way that they, they, they actually
are,

00:32:39.298 --> 00:32:41.388
those firms are actually very explicit
about how

00:32:41.388 --> 00:32:43.339
they think about Protestant markets, and
so they

00:32:43.339 --> 00:32:45.477
actually will have I think in each case,
they'll

00:32:45.477 --> 00:32:47.475
run an annual planning process, where they
will

00:32:47.475 --> 00:32:49.613
actually get together, you know, at the
beginning of

00:32:49.613 --> 00:32:51.565
each year, and they'll literally draw a
map

00:32:51.565 --> 00:32:53.970
of what they think markets are gonna look
like.

00:32:53.970 --> 00:32:56.230
And, so, you know, and, and it, it's
basically a value chain map.

00:32:56.230 --> 00:32:58.310
And, so, it's an interesting exercise to
think about.

00:32:58.310 --> 00:33:01.301
It's like, okay, like for example, for
networking, is that, like,

00:33:01.301 --> 00:33:04.778
fiber optics and communication chips, the
family of such things like routers,

00:33:04.778 --> 00:33:07.287
but, then we get into things like, you
know, ISP's and then

00:33:07.287 --> 00:33:09.073
ultimately lead into things like, you

00:33:09.073 --> 00:33:11.310
know, and other wireless businesses,
whatever.

00:33:11.310 --> 00:33:13.750
Kind of draw that entire thing out, as a
map.

00:33:13.750 --> 00:33:17.270
And then you basically have boxes for each
of the product, product categories.

00:33:17.270 --> 00:33:21.304
And then, you know, a VC firm can invest
in one company per category.

00:33:21.304 --> 00:33:23.900
And then basically the goal for the year
is to put a name in each box.

00:33:23.900 --> 00:33:26.460
And so they sort of consider it a success
at the end of the year

00:33:26.460 --> 00:33:29.020
if they've invested in the best possible
company

00:33:29.020 --> 00:33:31.660
they can in each box that they've
identified.

00:33:31.660 --> 00:33:34.060
That's kind of one extreme, we decided to
be more on

00:33:34.060 --> 00:33:36.460
the other extreme, which I think is a
little bit more what

00:33:36.460 --> 00:33:38.912
I would call the benchmark approach or, in
the old, in the

00:33:38.912 --> 00:33:43.180
prior generation, it would have been
called maybe the Arthur Rock approach.

00:33:43.180 --> 00:33:45.490
Which is basically, and, and by the way,
we

00:33:45.490 --> 00:33:48.300
have all the same theories, like we can't
help ourselves.

00:33:48.300 --> 00:33:50.520
We just sit around and talk about this
stuff all day.

00:33:50.520 --> 00:33:51.890
But, we need to climb more towards the

00:33:51.890 --> 00:33:55.030
other side, which is basically, the big
breakthrough ideas.

00:33:55.030 --> 00:33:57.920
The, the entire art of venture capital in
our view, is the big breakthrough ideas.

00:33:57.920 --> 00:34:00.179
The nature of the big breakthrough ideas
is

00:34:00.179 --> 00:34:03.190
that they're not that predictable and in
fact often

00:34:03.190 --> 00:34:07.704
upon first contact they seem nuts and it
actually turns out to be the case, now all

00:34:07.704 --> 00:34:11.530
the crazy ones also seem nuts so it's a
little bit of a you know they called

00:34:11.530 --> 00:34:14.229
Einstein crazy but they also called
Charles Manson

00:34:14.229 --> 00:34:17.580
crazy you have to be cautious on this
stuff.

00:34:17.580 --> 00:34:21.324
But, the really, really breakthrough ideas
often seem nuts the first time, the first

00:34:21.324 --> 00:34:24.060
time you see them and, and it's the fact
that they seem nuts, can be

00:34:24.060 --> 00:34:27.564
a very positive signal because number one,
it, that, that can explain why that thing

00:34:27.564 --> 00:34:29.196
already isn't being done by an existing

00:34:29.196 --> 00:34:31.425
big company, cuz it's just considered too
strange.

00:34:31.425 --> 00:34:34.612
And then number two, you know, if, if it
works, like if, if the bit flips

00:34:34.612 --> 00:34:37.870
at some point and it goes from being nuts
to being like, oh, that's a good idea.

00:34:37.870 --> 00:34:41.680
Like, then, you know, those are the
companies that could just explode.

00:34:41.680 --> 00:34:43.020
Could become just gigantically huge.

00:34:43.020 --> 00:34:44.700
And most of the, big ideas, the PC seemed
nuts

00:34:44.700 --> 00:34:47.580
at one point, the internet seemed nuts,
BitCoin today seems nuts.

00:34:47.580 --> 00:34:52.060
And, Airbnb seemed nuts Uber seemed nuts
in the beginning.

00:34:52.060 --> 00:34:54.370
And so you kinda wanna in, in our view we
have this

00:34:54.370 --> 00:34:58.020
sort of approach, you wanna kinda tilt
into the really radical ideas.

00:34:58.020 --> 00:35:01.030
But by their nature, you can't predict
what they're gonna be.

00:35:01.030 --> 00:35:05.300
And so what you basically wanna do is have
as prepared a mind as you possibly can.

00:35:05.300 --> 00:35:07.170
And learned as much as you can about as
many things as you can.

00:35:07.170 --> 00:35:09.289
And then basically enter as close to a zen

00:35:09.289 --> 00:35:11.844
like blank slate kind of state at the
beginning,

00:35:11.844 --> 00:35:13.899
you know kind of zen, you know set ideal

00:35:13.899 --> 00:35:18.174
of kinda perfect humility, which is hard
for venture capitalist.

00:35:18.174 --> 00:35:19.615
You know, sort of perfect humility at the

00:35:19.615 --> 00:35:21.480
beginning of the meaning basically saying
teach me.

00:35:22.550 --> 00:35:24.630
And then they either, you know, they
either do or they don't.

00:35:24.630 --> 00:35:27.430
But then, you know, the, the hope is, you
know, if Larry and Sergey walked in,

00:35:27.430 --> 00:35:31.460
and they're like, I know this is the 35th
search engine but this will be the one

00:35:31.460 --> 00:35:33.040
that works you know, you're open-minded
enough to

00:35:33.040 --> 00:35:34.860
say, you know, yeah, that might work, as

00:35:34.860 --> 00:35:36.320
opposed to, you idiots, don't you know
that

00:35:36.320 --> 00:35:38.916
that's been tried and failed so many times
before.

00:35:38.916 --> 00:35:41.898
And so we're way more on the side of we've
gotten sort of opportunistic

00:35:41.898 --> 00:35:43.437
trying really hard not to let ourselves

00:35:43.437 --> 00:35:45.360
be educated by the really smart
entrepreneurs.

00:35:46.390 --> 00:35:48.630
You asked about the best and worst pitch
meetings.

00:35:48.630 --> 00:35:51.490
The worst pitch meetings by far, are the
rip.

00:35:51.490 --> 00:35:53.740
And, I mean, we try really hard to not
have these get to us but.

00:35:53.740 --> 00:35:55.610
You know, snap trap for dogs.

00:35:55.610 --> 00:35:58.565
Like, it, it, you know, it's the I, I.

00:35:58.565 --> 00:36:00.760
[UNKNOWN] This is not a startup thing.

00:36:00.760 --> 00:36:01.560
I was giving an example.

00:36:01.560 --> 00:36:02.540
You see it in Hollywood, right?

00:36:02.540 --> 00:36:06.110
It's like one volcano movie works and then
there's like 400 volcano movies.

00:36:06.110 --> 00:36:08.572
It's like how many freaking volcanoes?

00:36:08.572 --> 00:36:10.540
It's like 35 of the top hundred games of
the iOS

00:36:10.540 --> 00:36:12.844
app store and now are like Flappy Bird
clones, like, and

00:36:12.844 --> 00:36:15.100
so and, and, and Paul Graham in, in, in
the adventure

00:36:15.100 --> 00:36:18.360
community Paul Graham calls this the
Hollywood approach to, to startups.

00:36:18.360 --> 00:36:22.000
Which is, it literally is, you know, it,
you know, is Airbnb for parrots.

00:36:22.000 --> 00:36:25.722
It's just, it's these infinite variations
of all the successful ones that you get.

00:36:25.722 --> 00:36:29.210
And by the way, the ones that sound silly
also just ones vertical search engines.

00:36:29.210 --> 00:36:30.764
When Google worked, so search engines went

00:36:30.764 --> 00:36:32.560
very deeply out of style when Google
worked.

00:36:32.560 --> 00:36:34.197
And then there were vertical search
engines in every

00:36:34.197 --> 00:36:36.440
single category, and, except for travel,
they all failed, right?

00:36:36.440 --> 00:36:38.970
Cuz it turns out there was just gonna be a
search engine.

00:36:38.970 --> 00:36:41.698
There wasn't gonna be a search engine for
health,

00:36:41.698 --> 00:36:44.050
there was just going to be a search
engine.

00:36:44.050 --> 00:36:45.890
and, so, it's all the variations and
clones and kind of, the

00:36:45.890 --> 00:36:48.250
mercenary kind of, kinda hit and run, you
know, kind of stuff.

00:36:48.250 --> 00:36:51.760
So, we, we try really hard not to sit in
those, because those are very painful.

00:36:51.760 --> 00:36:55.160
The ones that are the most exciting are
the ones where it's a, it's a

00:36:55.160 --> 00:36:57.410
really, really bright founder who's done a
tremendous

00:36:57.410 --> 00:37:00.384
amount of work and completely understands
the domain.

00:37:00.384 --> 00:37:02.361
And walks in with a really crazy idea and
then

00:37:02.361 --> 00:37:05.530
in the course of an hour, can basically
walk you through.

00:37:05.530 --> 00:37:09.045
Where we have this sort of concept we use
called the idea maze, which is the really

00:37:09.045 --> 00:37:11.373
bright founders with these really radical
ideas tend

00:37:11.373 --> 00:37:13.310
to go through what they call the idea
maze.

00:37:13.310 --> 00:37:14.975
So they tend to have worked for years.

00:37:14.975 --> 00:37:18.125
Working their way through the idea to try
to figure out how to get from kind of

00:37:18.125 --> 00:37:20.125
the initial crazy starting point to at the
end,

00:37:20.125 --> 00:37:22.530
something that will actually work in the
real world.

00:37:22.530 --> 00:37:24.610
And the really great entrepreneurs can
walk you through the

00:37:24.610 --> 00:37:27.390
idea maze and make you understand the flow
of thinking

00:37:27.390 --> 00:37:29.250
that got, got them to the point where they
actually

00:37:29.250 --> 00:37:31.120
came out the other end with what is a
great idea.

00:37:32.160 --> 00:37:35.853
And what's interesting about that is those
are generally not, there's this kind

00:37:35.853 --> 00:37:37.434
of you know, theory in venture

00:37:37.434 --> 00:37:40.100
capital that you want back coachable
entrepreneurs.

00:37:40.100 --> 00:37:43.644
The entrepreneurs who really have the
radical ideas are generally not

00:37:43.644 --> 00:37:47.966
in a way coachable, they generally react
with hostility to being coached.

00:37:47.966 --> 00:37:50.175
And so one of the things we test for, is
you

00:37:50.175 --> 00:37:54.350
know, basically say, have you thought
about doing it this other way?

00:37:54.350 --> 00:37:57.530
And what we're not looking for is the, oh,
that's a great idea.

00:37:57.530 --> 00:38:01.170
What we are looking for is the stare
that's just like, you idiot.

00:38:01.170 --> 00:38:01.620
Right?

00:38:01.620 --> 00:38:02.620
You moron.

00:38:02.620 --> 00:38:04.802
You've been sitting here listening, you
know, this is

00:38:04.802 --> 00:38:06.757
them to me, you've been sitting here
listening to

00:38:06.757 --> 00:38:08.438
me for 20 minutes and I've been working on

00:38:08.438 --> 00:38:10.528
this for five years, and you think you
understand this

00:38:10.528 --> 00:38:12.028
so well that you can make me a suggestion

00:38:12.028 --> 00:38:13.708
and not only are you an idiot for thinking
you

00:38:13.708 --> 00:38:16.919
can do that, but I will now explain to you
in detail why you're that big of an idiot.

00:38:16.919 --> 00:38:17.772
We love those.

00:38:17.772 --> 00:38:21.940
[LAUGH] Boy those are fantastic, those are
outstanding.

00:38:21.940 --> 00:38:23.743
and, so, and it goes right back to the

00:38:23.743 --> 00:38:26.302
combination of genius and courage I was
talking about.

00:38:26.302 --> 00:38:28.205
And so in particular I mean part it's of
us in the

00:38:28.205 --> 00:38:30.976
word, but part of us it's just the look on
the face.

00:38:30.976 --> 00:38:36.350
We love that look, it's like caviar.

00:38:36.350 --> 00:38:39.251
&gt;&gt; So I want to turn it over to Q and A,
so perhaps you

00:38:39.251 --> 00:38:42.082
can just finish with one question, which
is

00:38:42.082 --> 00:38:45.936
you had built two very, very successful
companies.

00:38:45.936 --> 00:38:49.006
And then in 2009 you decided to not run a
technology

00:38:49.006 --> 00:38:51.944
business, and instead found, what's become

00:38:51.944 --> 00:38:55.432
the fastest growing venture capital
business.

00:38:55.432 --> 00:38:58.540
People were probably telling you, you're
nuts, like you just said.

00:38:58.540 --> 00:38:59.050
&gt;&gt; Oh, yeah.

00:38:59.050 --> 00:38:59.910
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:38:59.910 --> 00:39:02.170
Well we went, we went, we went around to
see, we went around to see, cuz

00:39:02.170 --> 00:39:04.520
we'd worked with, we were working with
all, all the, all, most of the big, most

00:39:04.520 --> 00:39:06.450
of the big VCs, so we went around to see
all of our friends in venture

00:39:06.450 --> 00:39:08.410
capital, and tell them what we were
thinking,

00:39:08.410 --> 00:39:10.170
and they all told us we were nuts.

00:39:10.170 --> 00:39:12.680
Actually with two exceptions actually, two
of them are incredibly helpful,

00:39:12.680 --> 00:39:15.760
Jim Breyer and Neil [INAUDIBLE] were both
just tremendously, tremendously helpful.

00:39:15.760 --> 00:39:17.200
And we are very grateful to them for all
their help,

00:39:17.200 --> 00:39:19.560
but the rest of them pretty much told us
we were nuts.

00:39:19.560 --> 00:39:21.160
&gt;&gt; So what, what drove you?

00:39:21.160 --> 00:39:24.888
And, and perhaps you could answer in this
context when we apply to the GSP we all

00:39:24.888 --> 00:39:26.414
get asked to write an essay on the

00:39:26.414 --> 00:39:29.090
following question, what matters most to
you and why?

00:39:29.090 --> 00:39:31.500
So what today matters most to you and why?

00:39:31.500 --> 00:39:34.210
&gt;&gt; So we're really deep believers in the
power of technology.

00:39:34.210 --> 00:39:36.450
Like we, we, we, we think that the
technology industry has

00:39:36.450 --> 00:39:38.960
made the world a radically better place in
the last 70 years.

00:39:38.960 --> 00:39:40.820
And we think it will make the world a
radically better place

00:39:40.820 --> 00:39:43.440
yet in the next 30 years, and we think
it's just starting.

00:39:43.440 --> 00:39:44.800
You know, the fact that we've just now

00:39:44.800 --> 00:39:46.590
after seven years in the computer industry
gotten

00:39:46.590 --> 00:39:48.080
to the smart phone, which is the first

00:39:48.080 --> 00:39:50.090
computer that can get to everybody on the
planet.

00:39:50.090 --> 00:39:53.000
That everybody on the planet is gonna have
one of those.

00:39:53.000 --> 00:39:54.440
You know, that is a you, you know, we're

00:39:54.440 --> 00:39:56.210
just reaching the point now where we're
able to apply

00:39:56.210 --> 00:39:58.920
technology to a, a lot of really
fundamental problems

00:39:58.920 --> 00:40:01.590
in the world, a lot of fundamental
problems and opportunities.

00:40:01.590 --> 00:40:03.718
And so it, it, we, we just all feel or I,
I

00:40:03.718 --> 00:40:06.680
feel like we have spent, we, I spent my
career and then even

00:40:06.680 --> 00:40:09.175
previous generations of entrepreneurs the
value

00:40:09.175 --> 00:40:11.133
they have spent their career getting to

00:40:11.133 --> 00:40:13.990
the point where we can now do the things
that we can do.

00:40:13.990 --> 00:40:17.700
And so it's an amazing, it's an amazing,
amazing time in terms of what we can do.

00:40:17.700 --> 00:40:22.570
In terms of starting a venture capital
firm there is an aspect to starting

00:40:22.570 --> 00:40:24.440
there are some serial entrepreneurs who
like

00:40:24.440 --> 00:40:26.130
starting seven or eight companies in a
row.

00:40:26.130 --> 00:40:29.360
Dave Duffield is probably the best example
of this.

00:40:29.360 --> 00:40:31.940
I think [UNKNOWN] is company number 8 for
Dave which is amazing.

00:40:31.940 --> 00:40:33.990
I've done it three times, Ben did it
twice.

00:40:33.990 --> 00:40:38.228
Well, it depends how you count but twice,
two and a half times.

00:40:38.228 --> 00:40:40.075
it, for some of us at a certain point it
starts

00:40:40.075 --> 00:40:42.264
to be like, you know, it's like you climb
all the way

00:40:42.264 --> 00:40:43.870
up the hill and then you end up back at
the

00:40:43.870 --> 00:40:46.364
bottom of the hill and you have to start
the climb again.

00:40:46.364 --> 00:40:49.520
And so at some point you start to think,
maybe there's a way to contribute.

00:40:49.520 --> 00:40:51.394
That has to do with helping people climb
the

00:40:51.394 --> 00:40:53.829
hill, as opposed to being the person out
in front.

00:40:53.829 --> 00:40:55.693
And so I think we've reached that point.

00:40:55.693 --> 00:40:58.443
And we're very, you know, we're very happy
doing that.

00:40:58.443 --> 00:40:59.761
&gt;&gt; Thank you, thank you so much.

00:40:59.761 --> 00:41:02.184
Now we can have some smart questions.

00:41:02.184 --> 00:41:06.065
&gt;&gt; We're actually hosting the Future of
Media Conference here tomorrow.

00:41:06.065 --> 00:41:08.444
So I was interested to ask you about the
news business.

00:41:08.444 --> 00:41:12.626
Of course you've been tweeting and writing
and thinking a lot about that lately.

00:41:12.626 --> 00:41:16.513
And I guess if I could sum up your views,
your optimistic

00:41:16.513 --> 00:41:22.520
about the prospects for journalism to
thrive using a variety of business models.

00:41:22.520 --> 00:41:25.030
So I'm a former journalist and a lot of
the

00:41:25.030 --> 00:41:28.907
issues that you are thinking about now
have been being discussed

00:41:28.907 --> 00:41:31.992
within the industry for maybe eight or ten
years, a

00:41:31.992 --> 00:41:36.975
lot of you know, soul searching and, and
reflection and experimentation.

00:41:36.975 --> 00:41:42.105
So what I'm curious about is why did you
get interested in it now?

00:41:42.105 --> 00:41:44.798
Like what prompted you to get excited
about that

00:41:44.798 --> 00:41:47.638
space and to start thinking and talking
about it?

00:41:47.638 --> 00:41:49.495
&gt;&gt; Also, less from an investment
standpoint, cuz

00:41:49.495 --> 00:41:51.806
we don't really, content's not content,
generally, so

00:41:51.806 --> 00:41:53.527
we don't really, we're not gonna be making

00:41:53.527 --> 00:41:55.591
a lot of investments in, in media
production.

00:41:55.591 --> 00:41:57.637
Just cuz it's a, it's a different field.

00:41:57.637 --> 00:41:59.119
We, we can talk more about that.

00:41:59.119 --> 00:42:01.339
My interest in news, in media, is sort of
twofold.

00:42:01.339 --> 00:42:03.286
One is, as a, is a gigantic consumer of

00:42:03.286 --> 00:42:07.003
it, and somebody who thinks it's very
important, and then

00:42:07.003 --> 00:42:09.717
two, because everything I've worked on my
whole life

00:42:09.717 --> 00:42:13.383
gets constantly blamed, for the decline
and fall of journalism.

00:42:13.383 --> 00:42:13.919
so, the Internet.

