00:42:22.986 --> 00:42:25.583
so, at a certain point, I, you know, I
start to, I get,

00:42:25.583 --> 00:42:28.572
get, the, the, and, and, on behalf of all
the people I, I worked

00:42:28.572 --> 00:42:31.659
with, to, to, to build, to build things
like the web, it's like okay,

00:42:31.659 --> 00:42:35.926
maybe it's, maybe, maybe it's not the new
technology's fault at a certain point.

00:42:35.926 --> 00:42:38.387
So my observation is, and that I think
it's a very interesting

00:42:38.387 --> 00:42:40.429
topic, so I think the news, and I focused
on the news

00:42:40.429 --> 00:42:43.168
business, cuz that's kinda the, the, the
pointy end of the spear,

00:42:43.168 --> 00:42:46.200
in terms of, you know, the thing that
people are most worried about.

00:42:46.200 --> 00:42:48.020
I look at it as, as, I, I look at it,

00:42:48.020 --> 00:42:49.520
I do what very few people have been
willing to do,

00:42:49.520 --> 00:42:51.970
I think, which is look at it purely as a
business

00:42:51.970 --> 00:42:55.520
and basically say, and, and, so here's my
basic position is.

00:42:55.520 --> 00:42:59.936
Our view of what the news business is, and
about journalism is, is an artifact of a

00:42:59.936 --> 00:43:03.072
specific period of time, from 1945
basically into,

00:43:03.072 --> 00:43:08.290
basically 1945 to 2005, and basically
post-war post-war US.

00:43:08.290 --> 00:43:09.871
If you go back to the news business,

00:43:09.871 --> 00:43:11.875
before World War II, if you start in
Colonial

00:43:11.875 --> 00:43:13.613
days, and if you extend all the way

00:43:13.613 --> 00:43:16.910
to the 1930's, the news business worked
very differently.

00:43:16.910 --> 00:43:19.560
It was a very successful business and it
was, a lot of

00:43:19.560 --> 00:43:22.050
people were in it, a lot of people made a
lot of money.

00:43:22.050 --> 00:43:25.260
But like, as an example this whole idea of
objectivity, the journalist now take as

00:43:25.260 --> 00:43:28.730
kind of this, kind of, you know, kind of,
purer concept that has to be maintained.

00:43:28.730 --> 00:43:30.500
Like there was really no such thing.

00:43:30.500 --> 00:43:32.870
Like I always say like in in, sorry to
say, like objectivity's

00:43:32.870 --> 00:43:36.924
an artifact of an era in which news
businesses were monopolies or oligopolies.

00:43:36.924 --> 00:43:38.584
In the days when news businesses were

00:43:38.584 --> 00:43:40.950
fully competitive, subjectivity was out it
went.

00:43:40.950 --> 00:43:43.717
And they always say, you know, you're a
scumbag, how can you say that, and I'm

00:43:43.717 --> 00:43:45.445
like well Ben Franklin was a subjective
journalist

00:43:45.445 --> 00:43:47.820
and so stop calling Ben Franklin a
scumbag.

00:43:47.820 --> 00:43:49.260
You know, he actually knew what he was
doing.

00:43:49.260 --> 00:43:53.260
He was actually a very successful
journalist, a very successful publisher.

00:43:53.260 --> 00:43:55.380
There's a great book on the news business
in

00:43:55.380 --> 00:43:57.880
the Colonial era in the US called Infamous
Scribblers,

00:43:57.880 --> 00:43:59.860
which was what, it was a pejorative at the

00:43:59.860 --> 00:44:03.450
time for reporters, which could come back
into fashion.

00:44:03.450 --> 00:44:07.350
and, it's a great articulation of how the
news business actually grew up in the US.

00:44:07.350 --> 00:44:08.701
And then in the 20s and 30s, it

00:44:08.701 --> 00:44:11.650
got really interesting with figures like
Hurst and Pulitzer.

00:44:11.650 --> 00:44:13.730
And so, you can kinda, you can actually
study,

00:44:13.730 --> 00:44:16.400
kinda, the historical news business, kinda
through, I think through

00:44:16.400 --> 00:44:19.630
those two time periods and then you kinda
look at

00:44:19.630 --> 00:44:21.240
post World War II, and you say, well what
happened.

00:44:21.240 --> 00:44:25.023
Well monopolies and oligopolies got
established and so for and it, it was

00:44:25.023 --> 00:44:28.340
sort of this era of centralization in a
lot of parts of the economy.

00:44:28.340 --> 00:44:31.380
But it was very clear in the news
business, you had, you know,

00:44:31.380 --> 00:44:35.610
you'd have one major newspaper per metro
area, because of the cost of distribution.

00:44:35.610 --> 00:44:37.980
You'd have, you know, three TV networks,
nationally,

00:44:37.980 --> 00:44:40.500
because of the limited bandwidth for VHF
TV.

00:44:40.500 --> 00:44:43.260
You'd have, you know, a handful of local
radio stations.

00:44:43.260 --> 00:44:46.300
You'd have a handful you only had a
handful of magazines.

00:44:46.300 --> 00:44:49.420
You could only really have three general
news magazines on

00:44:49.420 --> 00:44:51.500
newsstands cuz you just couldn't afford to
distribute more than that.

00:44:51.500 --> 00:44:53.739
You scale, scale economics kinda ruled the
day.

00:44:54.800 --> 00:44:56.180
And so, so the news business went into
this

00:44:56.180 --> 00:44:57.750
mode where they kinda said, okay, we're a
monopoly.

00:44:57.750 --> 00:44:59.310
We're a monopoly or an oligopoly.

00:44:59.310 --> 00:45:00.394
And if you're a monopoly or an oligopoly,

00:45:00.394 --> 00:45:02.000
it's incredibly important to stay out of
antitrust trouble.

00:45:02.000 --> 00:45:03.571
And the best way to stay out of antitrust

00:45:03.571 --> 00:45:05.437
trouble is to not, to not make anybody
angry.

00:45:05.437 --> 00:45:07.372
And the best way not to make anybody
angry, is not have any opinions.

00:45:07.372 --> 00:45:09.340
And so therefore let's be objective about
everything.

00:45:09.340 --> 00:45:11.015
And then we can we can just basically

00:45:11.015 --> 00:45:13.733
say, First Amendment objectivity, don't
break us up.

00:45:13.733 --> 00:45:15.949
And that worked really well, as long

00:45:15.949 --> 00:45:19.347
as the distribution was controlled, which
it was.

00:45:19.347 --> 00:45:21.434
The distribution was locked down.

00:45:21.434 --> 00:45:23.412
And then the internet showed up.

00:45:23.412 --> 00:45:26.582
And then the internet introduced basically
took the legs out from under all the

00:45:26.582 --> 00:45:28.264
distribution monopolies and then all of a

00:45:28.264 --> 00:45:30.149
sudden there are you know millions of
voices.

00:45:30.149 --> 00:45:32.420
So my point as a business person is okay
that, that's

00:45:32.420 --> 00:45:35.660
the past, right, that's, that's, that's
the old days that's over.

00:45:35.660 --> 00:45:36.643
We need to look back to what

00:45:36.643 --> 00:45:39.200
happened when these things weren't
monopolies and oligopolies.

00:45:39.200 --> 00:45:40.712
We need to look back to the thirties and
twenties.

00:45:40.712 --> 00:45:43.574
And back to the colonial era, and we need
to basically think about

00:45:43.574 --> 00:45:45.187
how to build news businesses, media

00:45:45.187 --> 00:45:48.204
businesses, that thrive in a competitive
market.

00:45:48.204 --> 00:45:50.790
And that has to do with being, you know,
incredibly aggressive.

00:45:50.790 --> 00:45:54.000
That has to do with in many cases having a
very strong point of view.

00:45:54.000 --> 00:45:56.700
It has to with, not with the idea that
you're gonna be the only point

00:45:56.700 --> 00:45:59.542
of view, but you'll be one of many and you
have to argue things out.

00:45:59.542 --> 00:46:01.280
You have to have the right cost structure

00:46:01.280 --> 00:46:03.248
you have to think about market
segmentation and you

00:46:03.248 --> 00:46:04.389
have to, you have to do all the

00:46:04.389 --> 00:46:06.951
things that people do when they actually
build businesses.

00:46:06.951 --> 00:46:10.271
The issue in the news business is that a
lot of the executives in the business did

00:46:10.271 --> 00:46:12.032
not grow up in a competitive market and
and

00:46:12.032 --> 00:46:13.719
so they just don't know how to do that.

00:46:13.719 --> 00:46:16.468
So, now what's happening is, the new
entrepreneurs like Jonah Peretti

00:46:16.468 --> 00:46:18.591
